<!doctype html>
<html>
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<style>
  :root {
    --ink: #222;
    --muted: #777;
    --accent: #2b48c8;
    --cream: #faf8f4;
  }
  * { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }
  @page { size: 11.111in 8.333in; margin: 0; }
  body { font-family: Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif; color: var(--ink); }
  .page {
    width: 11.111in; height: 8.333in; overflow: hidden;
    page-break-after: always; position: relative;
    background: white; padding: 0.7in 0.9in 0.55in;
  }
  .eyebrow {
    font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--muted);
    margin-bottom: 4pt;
  }
  h1 {
    font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 30pt; margin-bottom: 14pt;
  }
  h1::before {
    content: ""; display: inline-block; width: 0.75in; height: 4pt;
    background: var(--ink); margin-right: 0.18in; vertical-align: 0.28em;
  }
  p { font-size: 11.5pt; line-height: 1.55; margin-bottom: 9pt; }
  .footer {
    position: absolute; bottom: 0.3in; left: 0; right: 0; text-align: center;
    font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 8pt;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em; color: var(--muted); text-transform: uppercase;
  }
  .footer .pageno {
    display: inline-block; background: var(--ink); color: white;
    border-radius: 50%; width: 14pt; height: 14pt; line-height: 14pt;
  }

  /* cover */
  .cover { background: var(--cream); padding: 0; }
  .cover .art {
    position: absolute; inset: 0; display: flex;
    align-items: center; justify-content: center;
  }
  .cover .art img { max-width: 62%; max-height: 82%; }
  .cover .titleblock { position: absolute; top: 0.7in; left: 0.8in; }
  .cover .titleblock .big {
    font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 44pt; line-height: 1.02; letter-spacing: 0.01em;
  }
  .cover .titleblock .sub {
    font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 12pt;
    color: var(--muted); margin-top: 6pt;
  }
  .cover .side {
    position: absolute; right: 0.35in; bottom: 0.5in;
    writing-mode: vertical-rl; font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
    font-size: 10pt; letter-spacing: 0.2em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  /* contents */
  .toc { columns: 2; column-gap: 0.8in; font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; }
  .toc div { font-size: 12pt; padding: 6pt 0; }
  .toc b { font-size: 14pt; margin-right: 8pt; }

  /* cards (tools explained / stitch guide) */
  .cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr); gap: 0.25in; }
  .cards.two { grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr); }
  .card { border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 0.18in; }
  .card h3 {
    font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 10.5pt;
    background: var(--ink); color: white; display: inline-block;
    padding: 2pt 8pt; margin-bottom: 6pt;
  }
  .card .sugg { font-style: italic; font-size: 9pt; color: var(--muted);
    margin-bottom: 5pt; text-decoration: underline; }
  .card p { font-size: 9.5pt; line-height: 1.45; margin-bottom: 5pt; }

  /* numbered columns (trace & transfer) */
  .numcols { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(5, 1fr); gap: 0.18in; }
  .numcols .col { border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 0.15in; }
  .numcols .col .n {
    font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 20pt; text-align: center; display: block; margin-bottom: 6pt;
  }
  .numcols .col p { font-size: 8.6pt; line-height: 1.4; }

  /* color guide */
  .colorguide { text-align: center; }
  .colorguide img { max-height: 6.1in; max-width: 100%; }

  /* materials */
  .materials { display: flex; gap: 0.6in; }
  .materials ul { font-size: 12pt; line-height: 2.0; margin-left: 0.3in; }
  .threadgrid { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    gap: 6pt 18pt; align-content: start; }
  .threadgrid .th { display: flex; align-items: center; gap: 8pt;
    font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 10pt; }
  .threadgrid .sw { width: 34pt; height: 16pt; border: 1px solid #444;
    display: inline-block; }

  /* steps */
  .steps { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 0.22in 0.45in; height: 5.9in; }
  .step { display: flex; gap: 0.2in; }
  .step img { width: 2.45in; height: 2.45in; object-fit: contain;
    border: 1px solid #e4ded4; background: var(--cream); }
  .step .txt .n { font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
    font-weight: 800; font-size: 16pt; display: block; margin-bottom: 4pt; }
  .step .txt p { font-size: 9.8pt; line-height: 1.45; }
  .step .txt b { font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; }
  .colorkey {
    position: absolute; bottom: 0.62in; left: 0.9in; right: 0.9in;
    display: flex; gap: 4pt; justify-content: center; align-items: center;
  }
  .colorkey .label { font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
    font-size: 7.5pt; letter-spacing: 0.1em; color: var(--muted);
    margin-right: 6pt; }
  .colorkey .k { font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 8pt;
    padding: 3pt 7pt; border: 1px solid #999; }

  .note { font-size: 9.5pt; font-style: italic; color: var(--muted); }
  .meta { font-family: Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 10.5pt;
    color: var(--muted); margin-bottom: 12pt; }
  .meta b { color: var(--ink); }
</style>
</head>
<body>

{# ---------------------------------------------------------- cover #}
<div class="page cover">
  <div class="art"><img src="{{ assets.color_mockup }}"></div>
  <div class="titleblock">
    <div class="big">PAINT WITH<br>THREAD</div>
    <div class="sub">A guide to contemporary needlepainting</div>
  </div>
  <div class="side">How to embroider — {{ project.title }}</div>
</div>

{# --------------------------------------------------- introduction #}
<div class="page">
  <div class="eyebrow">{{ project.title }}</div>
  <h1>Introduction</h1>
  <p>Hi there! Thank you so much for picking up this guide to contemporary
  needlepainting. Whether you are a newbie or an experienced stitcher, I hope
  you enjoy embroidering this design and learn something new along the way.</p>
  <p>This pattern requires only {{ palette.threads | length }} colours, but
  please feel free to experiment and use as many or as few as you like! The
  pattern comes with clear colour guides and instructions, but you are always
  encouraged to add your own personal touch. If you feel more comfortable
  using another technique, or want to use more than one strand of floss at a
  time, please do so! You don&rsquo;t even have to keep it in the hoop &mdash;
  you can embroider this design on your jeans, jackets, shirts, handbags,
  cushions, hats or whatever else takes your fancy.</p>
  <p>The embroidery technique used throughout is referred to as
  needlepainting, though you may know it as long and short stitch, thread
  painting or silk shading. It is essentially a very free and non-restrictive
  way to embroider once you get the hang of it.</p>
  <p>In an age where everything is instantaneous, it&rsquo;s important to take
  the time to create something with your own two hands. Embroidery encourages
  you to sit down, breathe, and ease into an evening (or a whole day!) of
  creativity and relaxation. I hope you fall in love with this ancient art and
  show the world how great something can be if you simply take the time to
  create it.</p>
  <div class="footer"><span class="pageno">2</span></div>
</div>

{# ------------------------------------------------------- contents #}
<div class="page">
  <div class="eyebrow">{{ project.title }}</div>
  <h1>Contents</h1>
  <div class="toc">
    {% for entry in toc %}
    <div><b>{{ '%02d' % entry.page }}</b> {{ entry.title }}</div>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>
  <div class="footer"><span class="pageno">3</span></div>
</div>

{# ------------------------------------------------ what you'll need #}
<div class="page">
  <div class="eyebrow">{{ project.title }}</div>
  <h1>What you will need</h1>
  <div class="materials">
    <ul>
      <li>A {{ hoop_display }} embroidery hoop</li>
      <li>Medium-weight fabric (cotton calico works great)</li>
      <li>{{ palette.threads | length }} colours of DMC six-strand floss &rarr;</li>
      <li>Embroidery needle(s), size 5&ndash;10</li>
      <li>A sharp 2B&ndash;6B pencil</li>
      <li>Fabric scissors &amp; floss scissors</li>
      <li>White tack and a printer (for the trace pattern)</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="threadgrid">
      {% for t in palette.threads %}
      <div class="th"><span class="sw" style="background: {{ t.hex }}"></span>
        <span><b>{{ t.code }}</b> &nbsp;{{ t.name }}</span></div>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="footer"><span class="pageno">4</span></div>
</div>

{# -------------------------------------------------- tools explained #}
<div class="page">
  <div class="eyebrow">{{ project.title }}</div>
  <h1>Tools explained</h1>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card"><h3>FABRIC</h3>
      <div class="sugg">Suggested: medium-weight cotton calico</div>
      <p>Choose a medium-weight fabric with a close weave &mdash; not
      transparent, but not so heavy that it is hard to pull your needle
      through. Avoid fabric with any stretch in it. If this is your first
      time, a light fabric makes it much easier to trace the pattern. Cotton
      and linen are affordable and look great; old pillowcases can be a great
      way to start.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>HOOP</h3>
      <div class="sugg">Suggested: {{ hoop_display }} wooden hoop</div>
      <p>The most important factor is that the hoop holds your fabric
      drum-tight. Wooden hoops grip the fabric well and the screw is sturdy
      and strong. If the fabric loosens whilst embroidering it can cause
      unsightly puckering; if you struggle to keep it tight, consider wrapping
      fabric tape around the inner hoop.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>THREAD</h3>
      <div class="sugg">Suggested: DMC stranded cotton</div>
      <p>DMC stranded cotton comes in around 500 colours and the sheen gives
      your finished embroidery a superior quality. There are six divisible
      strands, which lets you vary the weight of your stitches. Use one strand
      at a time for the finest blending; no more than two if you want extra
      speed. The {{ palette.threads | length }} colours for this pattern are
      listed on p.4.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>NEEDLE</h3>
      <div class="sugg">Suggested: size 5/10 embroidery needles</div>
      <p>If you are a beginner, use a needle with the smallest eye you feel
      comfortable threading. Look for needles with an elongated eye and a
      sharp point. As you get quicker at threading, go down in size &mdash;
      the thinner the needle, the easier it is to embroider through dense
      stitching.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>LIGHT</h3>
      <div class="sugg">Suggested: daylight lamp or magnifier</div>
      <p>An embroidery light isn&rsquo;t essential, but a white source of
      light takes the strain off your eyes and helps you see colours
      accurately. On a budget, a cool white/daylight bulb in an overhead lamp
      works just fine for stitching at night.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>PENCIL</h3>
      <div class="sugg">Suggested: extremely sharp 2B&ndash;6B pencil</div>
      <p>For transferring the pattern, a pencil is great because you can get a
      really sharp point, which helps with tracing the finer details. If you
      make a tiny mistake, dab some white tack onto the fabric to fade the
      line. A softer pencil shows up on fabric without having to press too
      hard.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="footer"><span class="pageno">5</span></div>
</div>

{# ---------------------------------------------- trace and transfer #}
<div class="page">
  <div class="eyebrow">{{ project.title }}</div>
  <h1>How to trace and transfer</h1>
  <div class="numcols">
    <div class="col"><span class="n">01</span>
      <p>Print the separate pattern PDF (the file with
      &lsquo;PRINT&rsquo; in the name) at <b>100% scale</b> &mdash; do not use
      &ldquo;fit to page&rdquo;. The dashed circle should measure
      {{ '%.0f' % hoop_mm }}mm across, matching your
      {{ hoop_display }} hoop.</p></div>
    <div class="col"><span class="n">02</span>
      <p>Cut along the dashed line so you are left with a circular piece of
      paper with the design in the centre.</p></div>
    <div class="col"><span class="n">03</span>
      <p>Cut a square of pre-ironed fabric roughly 2&ndash;3 inches larger
      than the hoop on all sides. Place the inner hoop down, lay the fabric
      over it, then press the outer hoop with the screw on top. Keep pulling
      the fabric on opposing sides while slowly tightening the screw until
      the fabric is extremely drum-tight.</p></div>
    <div class="col"><span class="n">04</span>
      <p>Place four tiny pieces of white tack on the corner marks on the
      front of the paper circle. Flip the hoop over and lightly press the
      paper, front side down, onto the back of the fabric. Hold it up to the
      light and adjust until the design sits in the centre.</p></div>
    <div class="col"><span class="n">05</span>
      <p>With the paper attached to the back, hold the hoop up to a lamp or
      window. Trace the outline you can see through the fabric with a sharp
      pencil. Don&rsquo;t move the paper once you begin. Remove the paper
      &mdash; you are ready to embroider!</p></div>
  </div>
  <p class="note" style="margin-top: 0.25in"><b>Tip:</b> the tracing process
  is more vital than you may think. If you rush this step, your finished
  embroidery will not be as high quality. Sharpen your pencil as soon as it
  starts to go blunt.</p>
  <div class="footer"><span class="pageno">6</span></div>
</div>

{# -------------------------------------------------------- fill map #}
<div class="page">
  <div class="eyebrow">{{ project.title }}</div>
  <h1>Fill map &amp; stitch direction</h1>
  <div class="colorguide"><img src="{{ assets.zonemap }}"></div>
  <p class="note" style="text-align:center; margin-top:0.15in">Each area is
  filled with its DMC colour and labelled with the code; the blue arrows
  show the direction to work your long and short stitch. Where two areas
  meet, blend the colours into one another rather than leaving a hard edge
  &mdash; use the thread colour guide overleaf as your shading reference.</p>
  <div class="footer"><span class="pageno">7</span></div>
</div>

{# ----------------------------------------------- thread colour guide #}
<div class="page">
  <div class="eyebrow">{{ project.title }}</div>
  <h1>Thread colour guide</h1>
  <div class="colorguide"><img src="{{ assets.color_guide }}"></div>
  <p class="note" style="text-align:center; margin-top:0.1in">The original
  artwork with each thread called out &mdash; your reference for how the
  colours shade and blend into one another as you fill each area.</p>
  <div class="footer"><span class="pageno">8</span></div>
</div>

{# ----------------------------------------------------- stitch guide #}
<div class="page">
  <div class="eyebrow">{{ project.title }}</div>
  <h1>Stitch guide</h1>
  <p>Long and short stitch is an embroidery technique that is fantastic for
  filling large surface areas of fabric. It is essentially like painting on a
  canvas, but rather than using paint, you are using thread. The shading is
  acquired by using a different coloured thread for subsequent rows.</p>
  <p>You are embroidering one short stitch, then one long stitch, in a series
  of rows &mdash; but don&rsquo;t worry about making each alternate stitch
  equal to the last. The more you stagger your stitches at different lengths,
  the better it looks: you want the longer stitches to be fighting to get
  into each other&rsquo;s rows. Ensure the stitches are tightly packed
  together and try not to overlap them &mdash; you don&rsquo;t want to see
  any spaces.</p>
  <p>Shading doesn&rsquo;t always follow straight lines. Where there is a
  curve, angle your rows to follow it, the same way water flows around a
  river bend. This is especially important with animals and plants: embroider
  with their natural curves and the motion of their fur or petals. If you get
  too caught up keeping everything neat and straight without changing
  direction, it will look unnatural.</p>
  <p class="note"><b>Tip:</b> when working the second or subsequent rows,
  bring your needle UP through the previous row and down into the unstitched
  fabric. Coming up from the unstitched fabric and down into the thread
  creates unsightly holes.</p>
  <div class="footer"><span class="pageno">9</span></div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div class="eyebrow">{{ project.title }}</div>
  <h1>Stitch guide p.2</h1>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card"><h3>LONG AND SHORT STITCH</h3>
      <p>Begin at the simplest place to start your first row. Thread your
      needle with a knot on the end and come up from the back to make your
      first stitch. Make another adjacent stitch with a different length.
      Continue until you have a row with no gaps, then work the next row in
      the same colour or a new one, always staggering the lengths.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>SPLIT STITCH</h3>
      <p>Split stitch follows a line and is great for outlines. Come up from
      the back and make one small stitch. Then come up in front of that
      stitch and go back down through the middle of it, splitting the thread.
      Keep the stitches as tiny as you can, especially on curves &mdash;
      that&rsquo;s the trick to making it look good.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>FRENCH KNOT</h3>
      <p>After anchoring your thread, pull it to the front and hold it tautly
      upwards with your free hand. Place the needle against the front of the
      thread and wind the thread around it twice. Still holding the thread
      taut, push the needle into the fabric as close as possible to where it
      came up, and pull through. Voila &mdash; a knot! Two strands of floss
      make a plumper knot.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="footer"><span class="pageno">10</span></div>
</div>

{# ------------------------------------------------ starting to stitch #}
<div class="page">
  <div class="eyebrow">{{ project.title }}</div>
  <h1>Starting to stitch</h1>
  <div class="cards two">
    <div class="card"><h3>DIVIDE THE STRANDS</h3>
      <p>Before you begin stitching, separate the six strands of thread:</p>
      <p><b>1.</b> Cut the length of thread you&rsquo;re going to use from
      the skein &mdash; about the length of your forearm.</p>
      <p><b>2.</b> Gently separate one strand of thread from the six, and you
      are ready to thread the needle. Use one strand at a time for the finest
      blending (two at most, or the embroidery can become
      &lsquo;lumpy&rsquo;).</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>THREAD THE NEEDLE</h3>
      <p><b>1.</b> Dampen the tip of the strand on a wet kitchen towel (or
      use your saliva!).</p>
      <p><b>2.</b> Hold the thread between thumb and index finger and pinch
      until you can barely see it.</p>
      <p><b>3.</b> Your fingers now support the thread rather than it being
      floppy &mdash; ease it through the eye of the needle. Don&rsquo;t knot
      the end near the needle; leave a couple of inches loose.</p>
      <p class="note">Tip: if the thread is frayed at the end, give it a
      little trim to help it go through easier.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>OPTION ONE &mdash; MAKE A KNOT</h3>
      <p>The simplest way to start and end your stitches, though knots can
      unravel over a very long time.</p>
      <p><b>1.</b> After threading your needle, create a tiny knot at the far
      end of the thread.</p>
      <p><b>2.</b> Embroider your stitches.</p>
      <p><b>3.</b> When you have nearly used up the thread, take it to the
      back of the hoop and knot it as close to the fabric as
      possible.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>OPTION TWO &mdash; ANCHOR THE THREAD</h3>
      <p>Worth learning if the piece will be washed or worn:</p>
      <p><b>1.</b> Choose a starting point <i>inside</i> an area you will
      cover with stitching.</p>
      <p><b>2.</b> Make the tiniest stitch you can, without a knot, leaving a
      tiny tail on the back.</p>
      <p><b>3.</b> Come back up near the first stitch and go down through the
      centre of it, splitting it &mdash; it should look like a tiny capital
      &ldquo;T&rdquo;.</p>
      <p><b>4.</b> Trim the tail and embroider away. Finish the same
      way.</p></div>
  </div>
  <div class="footer"><span class="pageno">11</span></div>
</div>

{# ----------------------------------------------------- step-by-step #}
{% for page in step_pages %}
<div class="page">
  <div class="eyebrow">{{ project.title }}</div>
  <h1>Step-by-step{% if not loop.first %} p.{{ loop.index }}{% endif %}</h1>
  {% if loop.first %}
  <div class="meta">Expected embroidery time until completion:
    <b>{{ time_estimate }}</b> &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; keep the colour guide on
    p.7 as a constant reference throughout.</div>
  {% endif %}
  <div class="steps">
    {% for step in page %}
    <div class="step">
      <img src="{{ step.png }}">
      <div class="txt">
        <span class="n">{{ '%02d' % step.n }}</span>
        <p>{{ step.html }}</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>
  <div class="colorkey">
    <span class="label">DMC colour key</span>
    {% for t in palette.threads %}
    <span class="k" style="background: {{ t.hex }};
      color: {{ '#fff' if t.dark else '#111' }}">{{ t.code }}</span>
    {% endfor %}
  </div>
  <div class="footer"><span class="pageno">{{ 11 + loop.index }}</span></div>
</div>
{% endfor %}

{# ---------------------------------------------------------- finish #}
<div class="page">
  <div class="eyebrow">{{ project.title }}</div>
  <h1>How to finish your hoop</h1>
  <div class="cards two">
    <div class="card"><h3>TRIM THE HOOP</h3>
      <p>Cut the excess fabric around the hoop, leaving roughly a one-inch
      border. Pinking shears reduce the chances of the fabric
      fraying.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><h3>THE DRAWSTRING METHOD</h3>
      <p><b>1.</b> Thread three strands of embroidery floss and tie a large
      knot at the end.</p>
      <p><b>2.</b> Cut enough floss to go around the whole circle.</p>
      <p><b>3.</b> Sew a simple running stitch around the whole border, then
      pull the thread tight (needle still attached) &mdash; it should all
      satisfyingly come together at the back.</p>
      <p><b>4.</b> Tie a sturdy knot so it doesn&rsquo;t come apart, and
      you&rsquo;re done!</p></div>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top: 0.3in">Congratulations on finishing
  <b>{{ project.title }}</b> &mdash; give yourself a big pat on the back for
  your patience and determination. I hope you enjoyed painting with thread!</p>
  <div class="footer"><span class="pageno">{{ 12 + step_pages | length }}</span></div>
</div>

</body>
</html>
